        "id": "ch17_7_1-1",
        "text": "Functions\nLike variables, functions are created to organize the code and avoid redundancies,\nmistakes, and programming bad practices. The code will look more readable\nChapter 17 I Programming with Python\n401\nand logical when we add functions. There are a few important rules that you\nneed to be aware of about using functions in Python, but first, let\u2019s look at how\na typical function structure looks:\ndef function_name(parameters):\n#logic goes here\nreturn [value/None]\nBased on the previous structure, here are the rules for creating a function in\nPython:\nI\nI Before the function name, you must add the def (definition) keyword.\nI\nI If your function will take parameters, then you must add them in paren-\ntheses after the function name.\nI\nI Before starting the function, you must add the colon ( : ) character.\nI\nI The function contents must be indented.\nI\nI Optionally you can add a return statement to inform that the function\nhas finished executing.\nLet\u2019s create a simple example by adding a function that will print any mes-\nsage that we will send to it:\ndef print_text (text_val):\nprint (text_val)\nreturn\nEsta secci\u00f3n contiene las siguientes subsecciones\n\u2022 Returning Values\n\u2022 Optional Arguments\nSi deseas explorar cualquiera de estas subsecciones, puedes pregunt\u00e1rmelo directamente.",
